@DHowett Releasing large buffers early would half the memory usage of conhost. But in case of `CP_UTF8` output it would still retain at least one maximum sized buffer for the lifetime of the process.
In my _personal_ opinion we should be able to just limit `CONSOLE_IO_RAW_WRITE` messages. As far as I can see it's exclusively sent for `NtWriteFile` calls, and those can be read with short reads. If we did that, we could ignore the fix for [2].
I can't estimate how important either of these fixes are, so I'm uncertain how we would want to approach this issue.

Like... what if there's a heuristic we could use to guess when to shrink.

```
// at the beginning of the conversion, so that we know how much space we estimated
if ((buffer.capacity() >> 1) > buffer.size()) {
    buffer.shrink_to_fit();
}
```

an application that consistently passes large buffers will make us hold our allocation, because we will need it again. an application that dumps text of varying lengths will eventually cause us to return that allocation and make a new, smaller one. It allows a poorly-behaved application to make us make a lot of transient allocations and discard them (write a large string, a small one, a large one, ...)... but _they're only making themselves suffer_ if they do that.
